IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `twa-dates`
IMPORT `twa-rules`

-- Wills Act 2008 (Tas) -- the questions of the PERSON CLAIMING UNDER OR AGAINST THE WILL of a person who has died.
--
-- SEAT: the beneficiary, the member of the family, the person who would share on an intestacy, the former spouse, the
-- witness who was given a gift, the grandchild whose parent died first: "Is the will valid?", "Can an unsigned document
-- count?", "Does my gift fail because I witnessed the will?", "Was the will revoked, by destruction, by marriage, by
-- divorce?", "Does the change on the will count?", "Can the will be rectified, and by when must I apply?", "Does the gift
-- fail because the beneficiary died within 30 days?", "Do I take my parent's share?", "How big is my share of the
-- residue now?", "May I see the will?", "Can I be paid maintenance in the first 30 days?". The will-maker in life, the
-- personal representative, the Court, the Tribunal and the Registrar get no seat: their findings are asked as facts.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, dates, numbers, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of twa-rules.l4.
-- Two DEONTIC rules (the witness's 6 months, s 13(2); the 3 months to apply for rectification, s 42(2)) are run inside
-- the questions that need them; no question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- The family provision claim itself (the Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912) and the intestacy rules (the
-- Intestacy Act 2010) are not in this Act.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (c) State of Tasmania, CC BY
-- 4.0; not the authorised version. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Wills Act 2008 (Tas): the claimant's questions`

§§ `Is the will valid`

@export Could the will give away this property (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 6)? Yes for property the will-maker was entitled to at death, even if acquired after the will was made, and for property the personal representative becomes entitled to as such after the death. No for property the will-maker held only as a trustee.
GIVEN `what kind of property is it` IS A `Kind of property` @desc The property you ask about: property the will-maker was entitled to at death; property the personal representative became entitled to, as personal representative, after the death; or property the will-maker held as trustee at death
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Could the will give away this property` MEANS
    `s 6 -- the will can dispose of the property` `what kind of property is it`

@export Was the will validly made, as to the will-maker's age and the signing and witnessing (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) ss 7, 8, 11, 20, 60)? A will made under 18 is not valid unless the will-maker was married, or made it in contemplation of a marriage that then took place, or made it under a Supreme Court order (with the Registrar as a witness), or it is valid under the law for minors' wills of the place where he or she lived. The will must be in writing, signed by the will-maker (or by someone in his or her presence and at his or her direction) meaning to execute it, with the signature made or acknowledged before 2 witnesses present together, at least 2 of whom could see it and signed in the will-maker's presence. A will signed and witnessed under the law of the place where it was made, or of the will-maker's domicile, habitual residence or nationality, also counts. If the answer is no, the Court may still accept the document (ask the next question).
GIVEN `how old was the will-maker when the will was made` IS A NUMBER @desc The will-maker's age in completed years on the day the will was made
      `was the will-maker married when the will was made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only matters if the will-maker was under 18: whether he or she was married when making the will
      `was the will made in contemplation of a marriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only matters if the will-maker was under 18: whether the will was made in contemplation of a particular marriage
      `did that marriage take place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only matters for a will made in contemplation of a marriage: whether that marriage took place
      `was the will made under a Supreme Court order authorising the minor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only matters if the will-maker was under 18: whether the Supreme Court authorised the will under section 20
      `was the Registrar one of the attesting witnesses` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a will made under a Supreme Court order for a minor: whether the Registrar of the Court was one of the witnesses
      `was the will made within the conditions of the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a will made under a Supreme Court order for a minor: whether it kept to every condition the Court set
      `was the will-maker a minor living elsewhere whose will is valid under that place's law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker died under 18 and the will is valid under the law for minors' wills of the place where he or she lived when making it
      `is the will in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will is written (by hand, typed or printed) on paper or other material
      `was it signed by the will-maker or by someone in the will-maker's presence and at his or her direction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker signed it, or someone else signed it for the will-maker in his or her presence and at his or her direction (the signature need not be at the foot)
      `was the signature made meaning to execute the will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the signature was made with the intention of executing the will
      `how many witnesses were present together when the will-maker signed or acknowledged the signature` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of witnesses who were all present at the same time when the will-maker signed, or acknowledged the signature
      `how many of them could see the signing and signed the will in the will-maker's presence` IS A NUMBER @desc Of those witnesses, how many were able to see that the will-maker signed and then signed the will in the will-maker's presence (they need not sign in each other's presence, or know it was a will)
      `was it signed and witnessed under the law of a place where it was made or the will-maker belonged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether its execution conforms to the law of the place where it was signed, or of the will-maker's domicile, habitual residence or nationality at signing or at death
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was the will validly made` MEANS
    `ss 7, 8, 20, 60 -- the will is validly made`
        (`ss 7, 20 -- the will-maker could make the will` `how old was the will-maker when the will was made` `was the will-maker married when the will was made` `was the will made in contemplation of a marriage` `did that marriage take place` `was the will made under a Supreme Court order authorising the minor` `was the Registrar one of the attesting witnesses` `was the will made within the conditions of the order`)
        `was the will-maker a minor living elsewhere whose will is valid under that place's law`
        (`s 8 -- the will is executed as the Act requires` `is the will in writing` `was it signed by the will-maker or by someone in the will-maker's presence and at his or her direction` `was the signature made meaning to execute the will` `how many witnesses were present together when the will-maker signed or acknowledged the signature` `how many of them could see the signing and signed the will in the will-maker's presence`)
        `was it signed and witnessed under the law of a place where it was made or the will-maker belonged`

@export Can a document that was not signed and witnessed properly still be the will of the person who died (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 10)? Yes if the document (or part of it) sets out the deceased's testamentary intentions and the Supreme Court is satisfied beyond reasonable doubt that the deceased meant it to be his or her will. The Court may look at evidence of how it was made and of the deceased's statements. The same applies to an informal alteration or revocation.
GIVEN `does the document set out what the deceased wanted done with his or her property after death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document, or part of it, purports to embody the deceased's testamentary intentions
      `is the Court satisfied beyond reasonable doubt that the deceased meant it to be his or her will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The Supreme Court's finding, if made: whether it is satisfied beyond reasonable doubt that the deceased intended the document to be his or her will
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the document count as the will` MEANS
    `s 10 -- the document constitutes the will` `does the document set out what the deceased wanted done with his or her property after death` `is the Court satisfied beyond reasonable doubt that the deceased meant it to be his or her will`

@export Is the will's gift to me void because I, or the person I claim through, witnessed the will (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) ss 12, 14)? A gift to a witness is void as to that witness and anyone claiming through him or her, unless more than 2 people witnessed the will and at least 2 of them were given nothing and are not the spouse of anyone given something; or everyone who would gain from the gift failing agrees in writing (with legal capacity); or the Supreme Court has ordered that the rule does not apply and the order has been served on the personal representative. A gift to a witness's spouse is not void.
GIVEN `what is my link to the witnessing` IS A `My link to the witnessing` @desc I attested the will; I claim under a person who attested the will; or I neither attested the will nor claim under a person who did
      `how many people witnessed the will` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of people who attested (witnessed) the will
      `how many of the witnesses were given nothing and are not the spouse of anyone given something` IS A NUMBER @desc Of the witnesses, how many were given no benefit by the will and are not the spouse or partner of anyone the will benefits
      `has everyone who would gain if my gift failed agreed in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether every person who would benefit directly if your gift were void has consented in writing to it being distributed under the will, and all of them have legal capacity
      `has the Court ordered that the rule does not apply to my gift and the order been served on the personal representative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has made an order under section 13 or 14 for you and a copy has been served on the executor or administrator
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my gift void because I witnessed the will` MEANS
    `s 12 -- the gift is void as to me` `what is my link to the witnessing` `how many people witnessed the will` `how many of the witnesses were given nothing and are not the spouse of anyone given something` `has everyone who would gain if my gift failed agreed in writing` `has the Court ordered that the rule does not apply to my gift and the order been served on the personal representative`

@export By when must I, as a witness given a gift by the will, apply to the Supreme Court to keep it (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 13(2))? The last day of the 6 months after the grant of probate (the same day of the month 6 months later). You may apply before the grant. The Court may extend the time, but no application may be made after the estate has been finally distributed.
GIVEN `when was probate granted` IS A DATE @desc The date of the grant of probate (or of administration with the will annexed)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply to keep my gift as a witness` MEANS
    `s 13(2) -- the last day of the 6 months after the grant` `when was probate granted`

@export Can the Supreme Court let me keep my gift as a witness on an application made on this day (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) ss 13, 14)? Yes if the application is in time (before the grant of probate, within 6 months after it, or later if the Court extends the time, but never after the estate has been finally distributed) and the Court is satisfied that the will-maker knew of and approved your gift and that it was not the result of fraud, duress or undue influence. You cannot rely on any presumption that the will-maker knew and approved. An order does not undo a distribution made before you applied.
GIVEN `has probate been granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate (or administration with the will annexed) has been granted
      `when was probate granted` IS A DATE @desc The date of the grant (any date if there has been no grant)
      `on what day do I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you make, or will make, the application to the Supreme Court
      `had the estate been finally distributed before I applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the final distribution of the estate was made before your application
      `does the Court extend the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only matters after the 6 months: whether the Supreme Court extends the time for your application
      `is the Court satisfied the will-maker knew of and approved my gift` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The Court's finding, if made: whether the will-maker knew of and approved your entitlement under the will
      `is the Court satisfied my gift was not the result of fraud, duress or undue influence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The Court's finding, if made: whether your gift was not included in the will through anyone's fraud, duress or undue influence
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court let me keep my gift as a witness` MEANS
    IF `it is no` `the timing`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE `s 14(1) -- the Court may declare that section 12 does not apply to me` `is the Court satisfied the will-maker knew of and approved my gift` `is the Court satisfied my gift was not the result of fraud, duress or undue influence`
    WHERE
        `the timing` MEANS `ss 13(2), (3) -- I may apply on that day` `has probate been granted` `when was probate granted` `on what day do I apply` `had the estate been finally distributed before I applied` `does the Court extend the time`

§§ `Was the will revoked or changed`

@export Has the will been revoked by what was done to it (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 15)? A will can be revoked only by a later will, by a writing declaring the intention to revoke signed and witnessed like a will, by a document the Court accepts under s 10, by a Court order for a minor or a person lacking capacity, by marriage or divorce (ask those questions), or by the will-maker (or someone in his or her presence and at his or her direction) destroying it meaning to revoke it, or writing on it or dealing with it so that the Court is satisfied from its state that he or she meant to revoke it. A change in the will-maker's circumstances does not revoke it.
GIVEN `what was done to the will` IS A `What was done to the will` @desc What happened to the will: a later will revoked it; a writing declaring an intention to revoke it was executed as a will must be; a document the Court accepts under section 10 revoked it; the will-maker burnt, tore or otherwise destroyed it; another person burnt, tore or destroyed it in the will-maker's presence and at his or her direction; another person burnt, tore or destroyed it, not in the will-maker's presence or not at his or her direction; the will-maker, or another person in his or her presence and at his or her direction, wrote on it or dealt with it; a Court order for a minor or for a person lacking capacity revoked it; the will-maker's circumstances changed; or nothing was done to it
      `did the will-maker mean to revoke it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For destruction: whether the will-maker intended, by destroying it, to revoke it
      `is the Court satisfied from the state of the will that the will-maker meant to revoke it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For writing on or dealing with the will: the Court's finding, if made, from the state of the will
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Has the will been revoked` MEANS
    `s 15 -- the will has been revoked` `what was done to the will` `did the will-maker mean to revoke it` `is the Court satisfied from the state of the will that the will-maker meant to revoke it`

@export Did the will-maker's marriage, or registration of a deed of relationship, after making the will revoke it as to what it gives me (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 16)? Yes, unless the will was made in contemplation of that marriage or registration (said in it or not), or is expressed to be made in contemplation of marriage or registration generally; or you are the person the will-maker was married to, or in the registered relationship with, at death (your gifts and appointments stand); or the will exercises a power of appointment over property that would not otherwise pass to the executor or administrator.
GIVEN `did the will-maker marry or register a deed of relationship after making the will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker married, or registered a deed of relationship under the Relationships Act 2003, after the will was made
      `was I the will-maker's spouse or registered partner by that marriage or deed at death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the person the will-maker was married to, or in the registered relationship with, when he or she died
      `does the will exercise a power of appointment over property that would not otherwise go to the executor or administrator` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was made in exercise of a power of appointment, over property that would not pass to the executor or administrator if the power were not exercised
      `was the will made in contemplation of that marriage or registration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was made in contemplation of that particular marriage or registration, whether or not the will says so
      `does the will say it is made in contemplation of marriage or registration generally` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will is expressed to be made in contemplation of marriage generally, or of registering a deed of relationship generally
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did the marriage revoke the will as to my benefit` MEANS
    `s 16 -- the will is revoked as to my benefit by the marriage or registration` `did the will-maker marry or register a deed of relationship after making the will` `was I the will-maker's spouse or registered partner by that marriage or deed at death` `does the will exercise a power of appointment over property that would not otherwise go to the executor or administrator` `was the will made in contemplation of that marriage or registration` `does the will say it is made in contemplation of marriage or registration generally`

@export I am the will-maker's former spouse or registered partner: does the end of our marriage or registered relationship revoke what the will gives me (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 17)? A divorce, a decree of nullity, a recognised foreign divorce or annulment, or the revocation of a registered deed of relationship revokes a gift to you, your appointment as executor, trustee, advisory trustee or guardian, and a power of appointment exercisable by you or in your favour, in a will that existed then; the will then works as if you had died before the will-maker. It does not revoke your appointment as trustee of property left on trust for beneficiaries who include your children, or a power exercisable by you only in favour of your children with the will-maker, or anything where the will shows, or it can otherwise be shown, that the will-maker intended otherwise. A separation without a divorce does not end the marriage; the Act does not deal with the end of an unregistered relationship.
GIVEN `how did our relationship end` IS A `How the relationship ended` @desc How the relationship ended: a divorce became absolute under the Family Law Act 1975; the Family Court granted a decree of nullity; it was dissolved or annulled outside Australia and that is recognised in Australia; it was dissolved or annulled outside Australia and that is not recognised in Australia; our registered deed of relationship was revoked under the Relationships Act 2003; we separated but the marriage was not dissolved; an unregistered significant relationship ended; or it has not ended
      `did the will exist when the relationship ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will had already been made when the marriage ended or the deed was revoked
      `what does the will give me` IS A `My benefit under the will` @desc What the will gives you: a gift of property; an appointment as executor, trustee, advisory trustee or guardian; an appointment as trustee of property left on trust for beneficiaries who include my children; a power of appointment exercisable by me or in my favour; or a power of appointment exercisable by me only in favour of children of whom the will-maker and I are both parents
      `does the will, or other proof, show the will-maker meant me to keep it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the will or can otherwise be established, as the Court would find it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the end of our relationship revoke what the will gives me` MEANS
    `s 17 -- the ending revokes my benefit` `how did our relationship end` `did the will exist when the relationship ended` `what does the will give me` `does the will, or other proof, show the will-maker meant me to keep it`

@export Does a change made on the will after it was signed count (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 18)? A crossing-out, insertion or other change counts if it was signed by the will-maker and witnessed as a will must be (the signatures may be in the margin near the change, or at a note referring to it), or was made under a Supreme Court order for a person lacking capacity, or the Court accepts it under s 10. Otherwise it does not count, except that words crossed out so that they can no longer be made out are gone.
GIVEN `was the change signed and witnessed as a will must be` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker signed and two witnesses attested the change, in the margin or near it, or at a memorandum referring to it
      `was the change made under a Supreme Court order for a person lacking capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the alteration was authorised by the Supreme Court under Part 3 Division 2
      `does the Court accept the change under section 10` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The Court's finding, if made: whether it is satisfied beyond reasonable doubt that the will-maker intended the change as an alteration of the will
      `can the words before the change still be made out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the original words, or their effect, are still apparent under the change
GIVETH AN `Effect of the alteration`
`Does the change on the will count` MEANS
    `s 18 -- the effect of the alteration` `was the change signed and witnessed as a will must be` `was the change made under a Supreme Court order for a person lacking capacity` `does the Court accept the change under section 10` `can the words before the change still be made out`

@export Has a revoked will been revived (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 19)? Yes if the will-maker re-executed it, or executed a later will showing an intention to revive it; a revived will counts as made on the day it was revived. The Act names no other way.
GIVEN `did the will-maker re-execute the revoked will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker signed and had the revoked will witnessed again
      `did the will-maker execute a later will showing an intention to revive it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a later, properly executed will shows an intention to revive the revoked will or part
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Has the revoked will been revived` MEANS
    `s 19 -- the revoked will is revived` `did the will-maker re-execute the revoked will` `did the will-maker execute a later will showing an intention to revive it`

@export Is a statutory will the Tribunal made for the person who died valid, given an earlier will (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) ss 31, 39)? No if the person had made an earlier will that was not itself a statutory will made by the Tribunal or the former Guardianship and Administration Board. Otherwise it has the same effect as a will the person made with capacity.
GIVEN `had the person made an earlier will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person for whom the statutory will was made had made a will before it
      `was that earlier will a statutory will made by the Tribunal or the former Board` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the earlier will was itself a statutory will made by the Tasmanian Civil and Administrative Tribunal or the former Guardianship and Administration Board
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the Tribunal's statutory will valid` MEANS
    `s 31 -- the Tribunal's statutory will is valid` `had the person made an earlier will` `was that earlier will a statutory will made by the Tribunal or the former Board`

§§ `Rectifying the will -- if the will does not say what the will-maker meant`

@export By when must I apply to the Supreme Court to rectify the will (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 42(2))? Within 3 months after the date of the death: the last day is the same day of the month 3 months later. The Court may extend the time, but not once the estate has been finally distributed.
GIVEN `when did the will-maker die` IS A DATE @desc The date of the will-maker's death
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply to rectify the will` MEANS
    `s 42(2) -- the last day of the 3 months after the death` `when did the will-maker die`

@export Can the Supreme Court rectify the will on my application made on this day (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 42)? Yes if the application is in time (within 3 months after the death, or later if the Court extends the time, which it cannot do once the estate has been finally distributed) and the Court is satisfied beyond reasonable doubt that the will does not carry out the will-maker's intentions because of a clerical error or because it does not give effect to his or her instructions.
GIVEN `when did the will-maker die` IS A DATE @desc The date of the will-maker's death
      `on what day do I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you make, or will make, the application to the Supreme Court
      `had the estate been finally distributed before I applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the final distribution of the estate was made before your application
      `does the Court extend the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only matters after the 3 months: whether the Court thinks an extension necessary and grants it
      `why does the will not carry out the will-maker's intentions` IS A `Why the will misses the will-maker's intentions` @desc The reason: a clerical error was made; the will does not give effect to the will-maker's instructions; or another reason
      `is the Court satisfied of that beyond reasonable doubt` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The Court's finding, if made: whether it is satisfied beyond reasonable doubt that the will fails to carry out the intentions for that reason
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court rectify the will on my application` MEANS
    IF NOT (`it is yes` `the timing`)
    THEN `the timing`
    ELSE `s 42(1) -- the Court may rectify the will` `why does the will not carry out the will-maker's intentions` `is the Court satisfied of that beyond reasonable doubt`
    WHERE
        `the timing` MEANS `ss 42(2), (3) -- my application to rectify is in time` `when did the will-maker die` `on what day do I apply` `had the estate been finally distributed before I applied` `does the Court extend the time`

@export Is the executor or administrator protected from liability for a distribution made before my rectification application (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 42(4))? Yes if it was a maintenance distribution under s 64, or if it was made at least 3 months after the death at a time when the personal representative did not know of any rectification or family maintenance application. Otherwise the Act does not say.
GIVEN `was it a maintenance distribution in the first 30 days` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the distribution was a maintenance distribution under section 64
      `did the personal representative know of a rectification or family maintenance application when distributing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when distributing, the executor or administrator knew of any application for rectification or any application under the Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1912
      `when did the will-maker die` IS A DATE @desc The date of the will-maker's death
      `when was the distribution made` IS A DATE @desc The date of the distribution
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the personal representative protected for the distribution` MEANS
    `s 42(4) -- the personal representative is not liable for the distribution` `was it a maintenance distribution in the first 30 days` `did the personal representative know of a rectification or family maintenance application when distributing` `when did the will-maker die` `when was the distribution made`

§§ `Gifts that fail -- if a beneficiary died, or a share fails`

@export Does the gift fail because the beneficiary died within 30 days after the will-maker (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 49)? Yes if the beneficiary died on any of the 30 days after the day of the will-maker's death: the will works as if the beneficiary had died just before the will-maker, so the gift does not go to the beneficiary's estate. No if the beneficiary lived past those 30 days, or the will shows a contrary intention (a general condition that the beneficiary survive the will-maker is not enough). A beneficiary who died before the will-maker, or the same day, is outside this section.
GIVEN `when did the will-maker die` IS A DATE @desc The date of the will-maker's death
      `when did the beneficiary die` IS A DATE @desc The date of the beneficiary's death
      `does the will show a contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will shows that the gift should take effect even if the beneficiary dies within 30 days (a general requirement to survive the will-maker does not count)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the gift fail because the beneficiary died within 30 days` MEANS
    `s 49 -- the will takes effect as if the beneficiary died first` `when did the will-maker die` `when did the beneficiary die` `does the will show a contrary intention`

@export Does a gift that failed fall into the residue of the estate (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 45)? Yes, unless the will shows a contrary intention. The section does not deal with the exercise of a power of appointment.
GIVEN `was the failed gift the exercise of a power of appointment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the gift that failed was the exercise by the will of a power of appointment
      `does the will show a contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will shows that a failed gift should not fall into the residue
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the failed gift fall into the residue` MEANS
    `s 45 -- the failed gift falls into the residue` `was the failed gift the exercise of a power of appointment` `does the will show a contrary intention`

@export My parent (or other ancestor) was given a gift by the will of his or her own parent or ancestor, and died before the will-maker or within 30 days after: do I take the share in his or her place (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 55)? Yes if the original beneficiary was the will-maker's child or other issue, the gift was not one that ends at the original beneficiary's death (such as a life interest) and was not a gift to the will-maker's issue generally, you would share in the original beneficiary's estate if he or she had died without a will leaving only descendants, and you survived the will-maker by 30 days. No if the original beneficiary failed a condition the will set, or the will shows a contrary intention (a general condition to survive or reach an age, or a gift as joint tenants, is not enough).
GIVEN `was the original beneficiary the will-maker's child or other issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person the will gave the gift to was the will-maker's child, grandchild or other descendant
      `would the original beneficiary's interest have lasted beyond his or her death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, under the will, the original beneficiary's interest does not end at or before his or her death (so not, for example, a life interest)
      `is the gift to the will-maker's issue without limitation as to remoteness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the gift is to the will-maker's issue (descendants) generally, without limit of generations
      `when did the will-maker die` IS A DATE @desc The date of the will-maker's death
      `when did the original beneficiary die` IS A DATE @desc The date of the original beneficiary's death (before or after the will-maker)
      `would I share in the original beneficiary's estate on an intestacy leaving only descendants` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, if the original beneficiary had died without a will leaving only descendants, you would take a share (under the Intestacy Act 2010); usually yes for a child, and for a grandchild whose own parent died first
      `did I survive the will-maker by 30 days` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were alive at the end of the 30th day after the will-maker's death
      `did the original beneficiary fail a condition the will set` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the original beneficiary did not fulfil a condition the will imposed on him or her
      `does the will show a contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will shows that the gift should not pass to the original beneficiary's descendants (a general survival or age condition, or a gift as joint tenants, is not enough)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do I take the share in place of the original beneficiary` MEANS
    `s 55 -- I take the original beneficiary's share` `was the original beneficiary the will-maker's child or other issue` `would the original beneficiary's interest have lasted beyond his or her death` `is the gift to the will-maker's issue without limitation as to remoteness` `when did the will-maker die` `when did the original beneficiary die` `would I share in the original beneficiary's estate on an intestacy leaving only descendants` `did I survive the will-maker by 30 days` `did the original beneficiary fail a condition the will set` `does the will show a contrary intention`

@export The will divides the estate (or its residue) into fractions and another fraction has failed: what fraction do I now take (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 56(2))? The failed fraction passes to the fractions that did not fail, in proportion; so your fraction becomes your fraction times all the fractions, divided by the fractions that did not fail. Not if the will shows a contrary intention.
GIVEN `what fraction does the will give me` IS A NUMBER @desc Your fraction of the whole or of the residue, as a number (for example 0.5 for a half)
      `what do all the fractions in the gift add up to` IS A NUMBER @desc The total of every fraction the will gives in that disposition, yours included (usually 1)
      `what do the fractions that failed add up to` IS A NUMBER @desc The total of the fractions that failed (not yours)
      `does the will show a contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will shows that a failed fraction should not pass to the others
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What fraction do I take after another fails` MEANS
    `s 56(2) -- my fraction after the failed parts pass` `what fraction does the will give me` `what do all the fractions in the gift add up to` `what do the fractions that failed add up to` `does the will show a contrary intention`

§§ `Seeing the will and early maintenance`

@export Am I entitled to inspect, and copy at my own expense, the will of the person who died (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 63)? Whoever has the will (including a revoked or purported will, or a copy) must let you if you are named or referred to in it; the surviving spouse, a parent or guardian, or a child or other descendant of the will-maker; someone who would share on an intestacy; a creditor or other claimant against the estate; a beneficiary under an earlier will; or a parent or guardian of a minor named in the will or who would share on an intestacy. No one may inspect it under this section while the will-maker is alive. Others may have rights under other laws.
GIVEN `has the will-maker died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker has died
      `what is my connection to the will` IS A `My connection to the will` @desc I am named or referred to in it; I am the will-maker's surviving spouse; I am a parent or guardian of the will-maker; I am the will-maker's child or other issue; I would share in the estate on an intestacy; I am a creditor or have another claim against the estate; I am a beneficiary under an earlier will; I am a parent or guardian of a minor named in the will or who would share on an intestacy; or none of these
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I entitled to inspect and copy the will` MEANS
    `s 63 -- I may inspect and copy the will` `has the will-maker died` `what is my connection to the will`

@export Can the executor or administrator pay me for my maintenance, support or education on this day, before my gift is final (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 64(1))? Yes if you were wholly or substantially dependent on the will-maker, your entitlement under the will becomes absolute only 30 days after the death, and the payment is made within those 30 days. The executor is not liable for it if made in good faith, even with a family maintenance application pending.
GIVEN `was I wholly or substantially dependent on the will-maker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were wholly or substantially dependent on the will-maker
      `does my entitlement become absolute only 30 days after the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your entitlement under the will does not become absolute until 30 days after the death (as under the 30-day survival rule)
      `is the payment for my maintenance, support or education` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the payment is for your maintenance, support or education
      `when did the will-maker die` IS A DATE @desc The date of the will-maker's death
      `on what day would the payment be made` IS A DATE @desc The day of the payment
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the executor pay me maintenance on that day` MEANS
    `s 64(1) -- the personal representative may pay me maintenance on that day` `was I wholly or substantially dependent on the will-maker` `does my entitlement become absolute only 30 days after the death` `is the payment for my maintenance, support or education` `when did the will-maker die` `on what day would the payment be made`

@export How much of the maintenance paid to me in the first 30 days is taken off my share of the estate (Wills Act 2008 (Tas) s 64(4))? All of it, if you survive the will-maker by 30 days. If you do not, none of it: it is treated as an administration expense of the estate.
GIVEN `how much maintenance was paid to me` IS A NUMBER @desc The total paid to you as maintenance, support or education distributions under section 64, in dollars
      `did I survive the will-maker by 30 days` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were alive at the end of the 30th day after the will-maker's death
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much maintenance is taken off my share` MEANS
    `s 64(4) -- the amount taken off my share` `how much maintenance was paid to me` `did I survive the will-maker by 30 days`
